  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates targeted alpha therapy at the cellular level. An antibody-like molecule carrying a radioactive payload is shown binding to a marker on the surface of a row of tumor cells. After the radionuclide decays, it emits several kinds of radiation with very different ranges and energies. The central emphasis is the alpha particle, depicted as a short-range, high-energy emission that travels only a very small distance, suggesting highly localized damage near the bound cell. In contrast, beta particles are shown traveling much farther, implying a broader but less localized effect across neighboring tissue. The decay also produces Auger electrons, labeled with very low energies, indicating extremely short-range interactions close to the decay site.\n\nOverall, the image compares how these emissions differ in penetration and likely biological impact: alpha particles deliver intense energy over a very short path, while beta particles spread farther, and Auger electrons act almost at the molecular scale. The row of cells and dashed arrows visually reinforce this contrast in travel distance, highlighting why targeted alpha emitters can be used to concentrate radiation damage on cancer cells while limiting exposure to more distant cells.",
